<article class="env-lemma" id="08YP">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/08YP">Lemma <span data-tag="08YP">10.14.4</span>.</a> Let $R \to S$ be a ring map. The functors $\text{Mod}_ S \to \text{Mod}_ R$, $N \mapsto N_ R$ (restriction) and $\text{Mod}_ R \to \text{Mod}_ S$, $M \mapsto \mathop{\mathrm{Hom}}\nolimits _ R(S, M)$ are adjoint functors. In a formula </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \mathop{\mathrm{Hom}}\nolimits _ R(N_ R, M) = \mathop{\mathrm{Hom}}\nolimits _ S(N, \mathop{\mathrm{Hom}}\nolimits _ R(S, M))  \]
</div>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      If $\alpha : N_ R \to M$ is an $R$-module map, then we define $\alpha ' : N \to \mathop{\mathrm{Hom}}\nolimits _ R(S, M)$ by the rule $\alpha '(n) = (s \mapsto \alpha (sn))$. If $\beta : N \to \mathop{\mathrm{Hom}}\nolimits _ R(S, M)$ is an $S$-module map, we define $\beta ' : N_ R \to M$ by the rule $\beta '(n) = \beta (n)(1)$. We omit the verification that these constructions are mutually inverse. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>